* SYNOPSIS

This is a quick architecture document to help understand the
internals of Lyntin.  Along with the online documentation (UML
diagrams and ool docs), this should provide you with everything
you need to know to build modules, fix Lyntin bugs, identify
problems, and hack Lyntin to your own devious desires.


* ARCHITECTURE

^<<
engine.Engine
   |-- 1 help.HelpManager
   |-- * session.Session
   |      |-- 1 net.SocketCommunicator
   |      |-- 1 alias.AliasManager           (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 action.ActionManager         (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 gag.GagManager               (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 highlight.HighlightManager   (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 substitute.SubstituteManager (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 variable.VariableManager     (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 ticker.Ticker                (not inherited from common)
   |
   |-- 1 history.HistoryManager
   |-- 1 threadmanager.ThreadManager
   |-- 1 ui.Baseui subclass
^>>

The engine holds the event queue and drives Lyntin.  Most Lyntin
things occur via an event starting with the StartupEvent and ending
with the ShutdownEvent.  The event queue is a synchronized queue
and thus gives us a centralized way of synchronizing all the threads
accessing Lyntin internals without causing them to block.

The engine holds:

	* the HistoryManager
	* the ThreadManager
	* the HelpManager
	* the list of Sessions
	* the user interface

All these things exist on the 'engine scoping'.

Each session has:

	* its own SocketCommunicator which abstracts socket
	  connections and network issues
	* an AliasManager which manages aliases
	* an ActionManager which manages actions
	* a GagManager which manages gags
	* a HighlightManager which manages highlights
	* a SubstituteManager which manages substitutes
	* a VariableManager which manages variables
	* a Ticker which allows you to do things at intervals

These things exist on a 'session scoping' in the sense that there
exists one of each of these for every session.


* MANAGERS

Managers manage things.  All the session-scoped managers subclass
the 'manager.Manager' class.  It doesn't really provide a lot of
functionality, but it does allow us to group them all and treat
them all the same.


* DATA HANDLING

Data comes from one of a couple of sources: the user or the mud.
User data (sometimes called Input) is received from the ui.BaseUI
subclasses and tossed into an 'event.InputEvent'.  Lyntin executes
that event and cycles through its input handling methodology.

Data can also come from the mud.  Mud data comes from the 
SocketCommunicator and is encapsulated in an 'event.MudEvent'.


* UI

All user interfaces extend the ui.BaseUI class in the ui module.
It has a series of methods which need to be overridden to give
the ui functionality.  In addition, ui's can specify additional
Lyntin commands, they can bind to Lyntin hooks (most ui's bind
to the STARTUPHOOK allowing them to perform certain functionality
on Lyntin startup rather than module loading time), and anything
else that modules can do.

Lyntin comes with a text ui which can be used over telnet/ssh
or in a command shell.  It also comes with a tk gui.  The tk gui
has functionality like split history screen (pgup and pgdown),
its own command history buffer (up and down) and numpad bindings
(VK_NUMPAD0 through VK_NUMPAD9).  There's also a very rudimentary
and unstable curses ui.

Feel free to extend the ui code and send in patches.  Or even
write your own ui based on another widget set or whatever you
want to do.


* HOOKS

The engine is augmented by a series of hooks which allow modules to
execute functions without having to change Lyntin's internals.  An
example of this would be the startup and shutdown hooks.  Any functions
that hook into these hooks will be executed upon startup or shutdown
of Lyntin.  Lyntin also uses these hooks to implement some of its
functionality.

For example, the TkGui uses the STARTUPHOOK to instantiate its gui 
interface and set itself up.


